## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamentals of memory allocators, pointer arithmetic, alignment, and simple free-list management in a low-level setting. It also checks the ability to reason about initialization and fragmentation.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator
- Free-list embedded pointers
- Heap initialization
- O(1) allocation/deallocation
- Fragmentation considerations with fixed-size pools

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define HEAP_SIZE (64*1024)
#define BLOCK_SIZE 32
#define BLOCKS (HEAP_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE)

static uint8_t heap[HEAP_SIZE];
static uint32_t free_head;

void init_heap(){
  free_head = 0;
  for(size_t i=0;i<BLOCKS;++i){
    uint32_t next = (i==BLOCKS-1) ? UINT32_MAX : (i+1);
    *((uint32_t*)(heap + i*BLOCK_SIZE)) = next;
  }
}

void* alloc_block(){
  if(free_head==UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t off = free_head;
  free_head = *((uint32_t*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE));
  return (void*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE);
}

void free_block(void* p){
  size_t off = ((uint8_t*)p - heap) / BLOCK_SIZE;
  *((uint32_t*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE)) = free_head;
  free_head = off;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to multiple threads? Consider a per-thread arena or atomic free-list head.

## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic operations, and memory ordering in a practical, portable way.

## Key Concepts

- Spinlock using atomic_flag
- Memory ordering: acquire on lock, release on unlock
- Exponential backoff with PAUSE to reduce contention
- Correctness test with concurrent increments

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { atomic_flag flag; } spinlock;

static inline void spinlock_init(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

static inline void spin_lock(spinlock* s) {
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    // small exponential backoff
    for (int b = 1; b < 1024; b <<= 1) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}

static inline void spin_unlock(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for NUMA systems to reduce cross-socket traffic?
- What are the trade-offs of using a ticket-lock vs this spinlock in high-contention workloads?

